- Set `translucent={true}` so the status bar overlays the app content seamlessly using the app's own background color underneath it, rather than showing a separate colored bar.
- Apply this globally in the root layout (`app/_layout.tsx`) via the `<StatusBar style="light" translucent backgroundColor="transparent" />` component so it's consistent across every screen, not set per-screen.
- On the Camera screen specifically, double-check the status bar still reads correctly over the live camera feed (light icons should still be visible over most camera content, but confirm no camera UI overlaps/gets hidden behind it — respect safe area insets there).
- Use `react-native-safe-area-context` (install if missing) and wrap the app in a `SafeAreaProvider`, using `useSafeAreaInsets()` on screens with custom headers so content never renders under the status bar or any notch/punch-hole camera area.

## 2. IMMERSIVE / HIDDEN ANDROID NAVIGATION BAR

Android SDK 57 supports edge-to-edge layouts natively; use the modern approach rather than a deprecated one:

- Install/configure `expo-navigation-bar` (`npx expo install expo-navigation-bar`).
- In `app.json`, ensure edge-to-edge is enabled for Android:
  ```json
  {
    "expo": {
      "android": {
        "edgeToEdgeEnabled": true
      }
    }
  }
  ```
- In the root layout, on mount (Android only, guard with `Platform.OS === 'android'`):
  - Set the navigation bar to match the dark theme: `NavigationBar.setBackgroundColorAsync('#09090B')` and `NavigationBar.setButtonStyleAsync('light')` as a baseline, so even when visible it blends with the app.
  - If the goal is a fully immersive experience (nav bar hidden until swiped up), use `NavigationBar.setVisibilityAsync('hidden')` combined with `NavigationBar.setBehaviorAsync('overlay-swipe')` so swiping from the bottom edge temporarily reveals it without permanently exiting immersive mode, then it auto-hides again.
  - Be aware: on gesture-navigation Android devices (no visible 3-button bar), this mostly affects the thin gesture pill at the bottom — test on both gesture-nav and 3-button-nav devices/emulators if possible, since behavior can differ slightly.
  - Do NOT hide the navigation bar in a way that makes the app hard to exit — always leave the gesture-swipe-to-reveal behavior intact rather than a fully locked immersive mode with no escape.
- iOS has no equivalent system nav bar to hide (the home indicator is separate and shouldn't be manipulated) — skip any iOS-specific handling here beyond normal safe-area respect.

